<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Life Adventures – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> That's a Small, Brown Bag.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Contribute to storytelling sessions with simple sentences based on visual prompts. Interactive: Use greetings and farewells appropriately in conversations with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can contribute simple sentences to storytelling sessions using visual prompts (e.g., "The book is big." when shown a picture of a large book). Can use appropriate greetings and farewells in brief peer interactions (e.g., "How are you?" "Bye," and "See you tomorrow."). Can ask and respond in a predictable pattern to questions about familiar objects (e.g., responding to "What color is the bag?" with "It's brown.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, using "Hello, how are you today?" and encourages choral responses like "Fine, thank you." Then, the teacher holds up real classroom objects (e.g., a big red book, a small brown bag, a blue pencil). Students call out the object names they remember from previous lessons. The teacher asks, "What's this?" and prompts for simple answers like "Book!" or "Bag!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a "small, brown bag" and says clearly, "This is a small, brown bag." The teacher points to a "big, blue book" and says, "That's a big, blue book." The teacher exaggerates pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and /s/ in "small" and /p/ in "pencil." Students repeat sentences chorally and individually, focusing on pronunciation and intonation. The teacher uses TPR by pointing to objects as described.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a small bag and a big book: "Is this bag big or small?" (Expected: "Small!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a brown bag and a blue book: "What color is the bag? Is it blue or brown?" (Expected: "Brown!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Life Adventures".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards of classroom objects (book, bag, pencil, desk, chair, eraser, ruler, board, marker, crayon) with varying colors and sizes. A simple, narrated story using the flashcards displayed on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing objects with detail: "This is a small bag." "That's a big, blue book." Responding to questions: "What color is the book?" "It’s blue."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher tells a simple story using the flashcards, for example: "Look! This is my backpack. It's big and red. In my backpack, I have a small, brown bag. And a long, yellow pencil. Oh, and a blue book! The book is big. It's on the table now." The teacher gestures and points to objects as described.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the story about? School things or animals?" (Expected: "School things!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher asks, "Is the bag blue?" (Shows brown bag). Students respond "False!" or "No!". "Is the book big?" (Shows big book). Students respond "True!" or "Yes!".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher holds up a flashcard, e.g., a "red pencil." Asks, "What color is the pencil?" Students say "Red!" (Chorally/individually).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Adjectives (brown, red, blue, big, small, long, short), Present simple ("That's a book."), WH-questions ("What color is...?"), nouns (book, bag, pencil, desk, chair, eraser).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Point and Say"</b> The teacher displays various classroom object flashcards or realia. The teacher points to an object, and students chorally and then individually say the full sentence: "That's a [color] [object]." (e.g., "That's a red book.", "That's a small bag."). The teacher corrects pronunciation of /b/ and /s/ as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill: "What Color Is It?"</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 3-4 object flashcards. Student A picks a card and asks, "What color is the [object]?" (e.g., "What color is the pencil?"). Student B responds, "It's [color]." (e.g., "It's yellow."). They swap roles. Teacher circulates, providing support and encouraging use of "please" and "thank you."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Describe My Object"</b> Students remain in pairs. Each student brings one or two personal school items (e.g., a pencil, an eraser, a small notebook). Student A describes one of their objects to Student B using two words (e.g., "Small, blue..."). Student B guesses the object: "Is it a small, blue book?" Student A confirms or denies: "Yes, it is!" or "No, it's not. Try again!" They then switch roles. Focus on "This is..." and "That's a..." with adjectives.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Life Adventures".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Pair storytelling with visual prompts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My School Bag Adventure"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small collection of 3-4 classroom object pictures (e.g., a big, red book; a small, brown bag; a long, green pencil; a white eraser).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A starts a simple "story" by picking one picture and saying two descriptive sentences about it, using demonstratives and adjectives. E.g., "Look! This is a big, red book. It's on the desk."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B then picks another picture and adds to the story, describing the object and its implied location or relationship to the first object. E.g., "And that's a small, brown bag. It's next to the book."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They continue until all pictures are used, creating a very short, collaborative narrative about objects in a school setting.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher encourages polite interaction: "Can you please show me the bag?" "Thank you for the book." and appropriate greetings/farewells if pairs interact with other groups briefly, e.g., "Hi, how are you?" "Bye, see you tomorrow."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes students during the "My School Bag Adventure" activity (Stage 4). Individually, each student then describes one classroom object they see, using a complete sentence. (e.g., pointing to a red crayon, "That's a red crayon.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (/b/ in "book", /s/ in "small", /p/ in "pencil").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (at least 3 nouns and 2 adjectives, e.g., "big," "small," "brown," "red," "blue").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of simple sentences:</b> (e.g., "This is a small bag.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and polite expressions:</b> (e.g., attempts "Thank you," "Please," "How are you?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students orally complete one of the following before leaving the activity area:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say: That's a ___ ___." (e.g., "That's a big book.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My favorite color for a bag is ___."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today about school things?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like talking about the objects with your friend?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- What worked well in terms of student speaking output and interaction? Most students were able to use simple sentences like "It's brown" and "That's a big book." The "My School Bag Adventure" activity engaged them in peer conversation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing)? Some students still need more practice with subject-verb agreement (e.g., "It is" vs. "It's"). For writing, I'll need to provide sentence frames and clear models for constructing simple descriptive sentences based on objects, building on the oral practice from this lesson.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson provided the oral foundation for describing objects with adjectives and simple sentences. In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will transfer these spoken descriptions into written form, practicing writing simple sentences like "This is a small, brown bag." and "It is red."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>